d anyone  want to revert back to that the end of  this poem however is one of  hope that America will one day be that  idealized vision of freedom equality and  opportunity we're not there yet but we  can dream that we one day will the  introduction is laden with examples of  both military and police oppression the  history of America through the lens of  Spike Lee is one of subjugation violence  and injustice both internally and abroad  seeing these images do we want to make  America great again and for whom are we  making America great for it's easy to be  pessimistic about the world given this  introduction but as we'll find out as  the film unfolds Lee leans into hope  just like dr. King the film may begin  with pessimism but ends with hope in  modern-day Vietnam we meet our four  protagonists Paul Otis Eddie and Melvin  they've teamed up to search for a stash  of gold they buried almost 50 years ago  this gold was originally meant for the  lahu people a gift from the Americans  for signing with them during the war but  the Bloods buried it hoping one day to  come back and use the money to help  their own 1619 this speech is given by  Norman the fifth member of the five  Bloods and their leader  the Bloods aren't just looking for the  gold but also Norman's body so that he  may be brought home and given a proper  burial of all the men Paul had the  closest connection to Norman which is  odd since Paul goes against almost  everything Norman stands for when the  bloods here that dr. King has been  assassinated Paul calls her blood Norman  however invokes King saying that they  need to control their rage and be  peaceful when Norman says the gold  should be used for the betterment of  their people  Paul leader says the money will be spent  on himself that goal should go towards  Black Liberation you do for self Norman  is seen as this saint-like figure in one  scene even sitting on a makeshift throne  made out of palm branches in Christian  theology palm branches symbolized  victory triumph and peace all things  Norman represents this is juxtaposed  with  who symbolizes greed violence and rage  Paul's story is one of tragedy he served  three tours in Vietnam killed his best  friend by friendly fire a secret he  keeps hidden that Fester's inside of him  he also watched as his wife died giving  birth to his son David and has a  complicated and dysfunctional  relationship with him David is also a  foil to his father  whereas Paul is uneducated David went to  college to become a teacher David wanted  to become the opposite of what his  father was in a sub conscious effort to  lash out at his father who never loved  him Hall even chooses abandoning his son  who is just shot in order to leave in  the jungle on his own with his share of  the gold Paul also wears a make America  great again hat completely oblivious to  the effect it would have on the people  there a country which lost millions of  lives due to American military  intervention during the war but this hat  becomes a metaphor for greed throughout  the film in fact the only characters who  ever wear the hat are all deeply flawed  greedy and violent individuals we have  Paul and the European gold smuggler day  rush fun fact the French translation of  day rush is rocks the same thing that  comes out of otis 'as matt pack when  they trick him at the end of the film  it's no coincidence this had a symbolic  of greed considering Spike Lee's vocal  criticism of US President Donald Trump  who adopted the slogan make America  great again for his 2016 presidential  campaign it's pretty clear in the film  what spike thinks of the current US  president I guess you're too busy  watching reality TV or electing a  reality TV clown for a president so  charges claims accusations allegations  in a  oceans connected to the clansmen an oval  office Forli manga greed and trump go  hand in hand  and Trump himself has been very vocal  about how greed has been one of the  driving forces of his life because my  whole life I've been greedy greedy  greedy I've scraped all the money I  could get I'm so greedy but now I want  to be greedy for the United States I  want to grab all that money  Paul's Ark however isn't without some  form of redemption after bitten by a  snake Paul confronts the ghost of Norman  a ghost he doesn't want to see away men  please he doesn't want to confront the  thing that has caused him so much pain  all these years the thing that's given  him nightmares every night by keeping  the secret that he killed Norman buried  inside him he's let it fester making the  pain inside him lash out to those he  loves including his son it's here that  Norman tells Paul what he's needed to  learn all these years that it wasn't his  fault and that Norman forgives him  Norman's forgiveness is in line with his  almost christ-like character he even  says that he died for Paul just like  Christ died for our sins I know you  while Paul and Norman are  philosophically at two ends of the  spectrum  Otis is caught in the middle swaying  back and forth between these two men as  if caught in a tide near the end of the  film Otis is shot by des Ross who throws  the make America great again hat onto  his body and when des rushes Shawn we  get this madness laughter and madness  this whole situation is so out of hand  Otis can't help but laugh and I can't  help but think this is Spike Lee  commentating on the state of America  today the manga hat on full display over  the body of a dying black man well in  Vietnam  Otis fell in love with a prostitute  named Thien and unbeknownst to him  fathered a daughter these children of  American soldiers became known as Bui  doy or children of the dust they were  mocked and looked down in Vietnamese  Society for being the sons and daughters  of sex workers and looking like the  enemy no one knows the exact number of  half a mare  can half-asian children were born during  the war but the estimates are in the  tens of thousands part of Otis's journey  is cultivating the relationship with his  daughter who at the end of the film he  embraces finally happy and the film ends  largely on a positive note as the Bloods  are able to get the money for their gold  and put it to good use  we see here Melvin's wife [ __ ] and  their son she's received a check for two  million dollars at the beginning of the  film Melvin tells the Bloods that she  wants to move out of the neighborhood  ever since a group of thugs moved in so  this money will absolutely help for that  we also see her holding a Purple Heart  but we aren't sure if that was  previously earned or somehow  posthumously awarded for his valiant  show of bravery taking a grenade for his  fellow men at a black lives matter  headquarters one of the organizers says  they just received a check for two  million dollars this check is from Eddie  one of the few voices who said the money  should go back to the people  in a similar vein Hedy Simon and seppo  share has gone towards the fight against  landmines Eddie's life has been devoted  to helping those affected by war she  feels somewhat responsible for what has  happened in countries like Vietnam  because her ancestors were complicit in  the exploitation of the Vietnamese  people the French had colonized Vietnam  and her family made a fortune off the  various rubber plantations and Bryce  farms which utilized Vietnamese labour  this is her way of paying back then even  gets his share which judging by the  posters in the back of his agency looks  to have gone towards his business we  also get a short epilogue featuring  Norman's two sisters as his body is  brought back to US soil this is followed  by David at a school where he teaches  African American history the board  behind him is filled with various black  historical figures but this whole  epilogue is narrated by Paul in his  final redemptive moment  this is him reciting his letter to David  a letter he wrote before the main  offense of the film and only to be read  upon his death in it he details how the  demons of his past haunted him and how  that pain was taken out on his son he  acknowledges how he made David feel like  he wasn't loved how he failed him as a  father and to let him know it wasn't his  son's fault there are small interesting  details here like the empty bottle of  Walker and the pictures of his family in  the background this was a deeply  troubled man but one who deep down loved  his family even if he rarely showed it  you best believe the film ends with the  words of Langston Hughes recited by dr.  King with that hope of a better America  but as the final text scroll indicates  we are reminded we are still far from it  dr. King a man of peace was shot for  standing up for what he believed in and  if there's anything recent events  surrounding George Floyd and the BLM  protests have shown us is that things  are still far from that idealized  American dream of freedom and equality  the real treasure of defied Bloods  ultimately had nothing to do with gold  it's forming these new relationships  love and forgiveness until we as a  society learn this too we still have a  long way to go thanks for watching  everyone if you liked this video please  make sure to LIKE and subscribe I'm  always coming out with new videos you  can also follow me on twitter at things  story whitey
 

